// XFAIL: DF-235a a fixed-array literal element that is a constant
// EXPRESSION (not a bare integer literal) does not adopt the array's
// declared width, and mixing it with an element that DOES adopt (a bare
// literal) throws an internal compiler error at codegen — in EITHER
// element order.
//
// Found while building design 235's coercion-adoption grid, testing the
// array-element position against a const-expression source (`(1 << 3) |
// (1 << 4)`, design 185's folded shift/bitwise family).
//
// MECHANISM (obligation 4): `_check_array_literal`
// (sawc/typechecker/expressions.py:4749) checks each element via
// `_check_expression` + `_element_fits`, but — unlike its sibling
// `_check_repeat_literal` (expressions.py:4852, which explicitly reassigns
// `elem_type = arr_elem` at line 4893 once an element fits) — never coerces
// a non-`IntLiteral` element's CHECKED type to the array's DECLARED element
// type. The pre-pass that would normally do this, `_apply_literal_expected_type`
// (its ArrayLiteral case, expressions.py:5374-5392), only stamps an element
// whose NODE SHAPE it dispatches on (a bare `IntLiteral`, unary-minus,
// if/match/block, a nested Tuple/Array/Map/Set literal, `None`, or a
// FuncPointer-shaped closure/name) — a `BinaryOp` constant expression has no
// matching case, so it is checked with NO expected width at all and defaults
// to platform `Int`.
//
// TWO SYMPTOMS, one root cause. (1) A UNIFORM array of const-expression
// elements silently stores at the WRONG width with no range check against
// the declared narrower type — `let a: [UInt32; 1] = [1 << 40]` compiles
// clean and prints `1099511627776`, the untruncated platform-`Int` value,
// though `UInt32` can hold none of it (see
// array_literal_const_element_range_check.saw). (2) A MIXED array — one
// element that adopts (a bare literal, typed via the funnel above) beside
// one that does not (a const expression, left at platform `Int`) — reaches
// codegen's `_generate_array_literal` (sawc/codegen/collections.py:159-161,
// `elem_type = element_values[0].type` — the array's LLVM element type is
// derived from element 0's OWN codegen'd type, not from the array's declared
// Saw type) with disagreeing element widths, and `insert_value` throws:
//
//   error: internal compiler error ... (ArrayLiteral): Can only insert i64
//   at [1] in [2 x i64]: got i32
//
// (the reverse order ICEs the same way, with i32/i64 swapped).
//
// SWEEP (obligation 4, compile evidence): the SAME mixed elements
// (`[0, (1 << 3) | (1 << 4)]`) into a `Vector<UInt32>` and a
// `(UInt32, UInt32)` tuple both compile AND print the correct `0 24` —
// Vector's codegen builds via per-element `push` and Tuple's codegen
// addresses each slot by its OWN declared type, neither deriving from
// element 0's codegen'd type, so neither ICEs. The repeat literal `[v; N]`
// does not ICE either, but it is NOT immune to the underlying gap — it has
// no SECOND element to disagree with, so it silently carries the wrong
// width instead of crashing (DF-235b, const_expression_range_unchecked_wide.saw).
// So the CRASH shown here is isolated to the PLAIN (non-repeat) fixed-array
// literal's typechecking + codegen pair — but the underlying "const
// expression never adopts" gap reaches every position in DF-235b's matrix.
//
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// 0 24

func main() {
    let a: [UInt32; 2] = [0, (1 << 3) | (1 << 4)]
    print("{a[0]} {a[1]}")
}
